    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. so if the lisinopril is blocking that enzyme, how does that affect the actual concentration of angiotensin i? does it just stay at higher levels in the blood or something? could u go into a bit more detail on that specific part of the pathway?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Certainly! When lisinopril inhibits the angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) that acts on angiotensin I (Ang I), it prevents the conversion of Ang I to angiotensin II (Ang II). Ang I is a relatively inactive decapeptide that circulates where it is converted mainly in the lungs by the aforementioned epithelium-harboring ACE. Consequently, in the presence of lisinopril, more angiotensin I remains in the bloodstream, and its concentration does not necessarily stay high overall because its conversion to angiotensin II (a more potent vasoconstrictor) is hampered. Ang II is short-lived due to rapid enzymatic degradation, leading readily to its inactive metabolite, angiotensin 1-7, which can ultimately exert opposing vasodilatory impacts; thus, blocking ACE mainly affects the levels of angiotensin II without the dramatic elevation of Ang I across the systemic circulation.",
      "source": "target",
